  "id": "Compound::DB08899 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Enzalutamide, a well-established androgen receptor antagonist, is implicated in the regulation of Cyp2c19 through a series of mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Enzalutamide may interact with Cyp2c19 via a shared pathway involving androgen receptor inhibition. This is supported by the inclusion of Enzalutamide in the Androgen Receptor Antagonists class, which includes Bicalutamide and Nilutamide, both of which bind to Cyp2c19. The lowest common ancestor of these compounds points to the Androgen Receptor Inhibitor, reinforcing the notion that the interaction with Cyp2c19 is mediated through a common biological mechanism.\n\nThe evidence also indicates that Enzalutamide binds to Cyp2c8, which is involved in steroid hydroxylase activity, and this activity is ultimately linked to Cyp2c19. This suggests a potential indirect pathway where Enzalutamide may influence Cyp2c19 through its effects on Cyp2c8, though the direct interaction remains unclear. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, but the evidence is not definitive. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of androgen receptor inhibition in modulating Cyp2c19 activity, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mechanism.",
